## 📐 Part III: Technical Appendix## 🧮 The 5-Sigma Hubble Tension Calculation
The cosmological crisis manifests as a severe statistical divergence between two independent methodologies calculating the Hubble constant ($H_0$).
## 1. The Inverse Distance Ladder (Early Universe)

* Data Source: Planck Satellite Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) angular power spectrum.
* Baseline Model: Standard $\Lambda$CDM framework.
* Calculated Value:
$$H_0 = 67.4 \pm 0.5 \text{ km/s/Mpc}$$

## 2. The Direct Distance Ladder (Late Universe)

* Data Source: H0DN Collaboration tracking Cepheid variables and Type Ia Supernovae.
* Methodology: Geometrical parallax anchoring absolute luminosity distances.
* Calculated Value:
$$H_0 = 73.5 \pm 0.8 \text{ km/s/Mpc}$$

## 3. Statistical Significance ($\sigma$)
The significance of the mismatch is calculated using the standard error variance formula:
$$\sigma_{\text{tension}} = \frac{|H_{0, \text{local}} - H_{0, \text{early}}|}{\sqrt{\sigma_{\text{local}}^2 + \sigma_{\text{early}}^2}}$$
Substituting the empirical values:
$$\sigma_{\text{tension}} = \frac{|73.5 - 67.4|}{\sqrt{0.8^2 + 0.5^2}} = \frac{6.1}{\sqrt{0.64 + 0.25}} = \frac{6.1}{\sqrt{0.89}} \approx \frac{6.1}{0.943} \approx 6.46\sigma$$

* The Context: A divergence exceeding $5\sigma$ represents a less than 1-in-3.5 million chance of being a fluke.
* The Implication: This formal mathematical mismatch proves that the standard model cannot simultaneously accommodate both cosmic horizons without a systemic structural failure.

